<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">12 August in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 12 August, from 1099 to 2000.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1099">1099 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">927 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Crusaders defeat a Fatimid army at Ascalon</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A month after taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, the crusaders surprised and routed an Egyptian relief army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a>. The victory, the last battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, secured the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> kingdom.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">1121 CE <span class="tih-ago">905 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">David IV of Georgia defeats a Seljuk-led army at Didgori</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">At Didgori, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> IV the Builder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> defeated a large Seljuk-led Muslim army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a>. The victory allowed David to take Tbilisi in 1122 and make it his capital.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-didgori">Battle of Didgori</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-iv-of-georgia">David IV of Georgia</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilghazi">Ilghazi</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1204">1204 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">822 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Venice acquires Crete from Boniface of Montferrat</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Venetians acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface of Montferrat</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, forestalling their Genoese rivals. Boniface sold it for 1,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> marks, a share of the island's revenues and Venetian support for his bid for the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boniface-i-marquis-of-montferrat">Boniface I, Marquis of Montferrat</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1323">1323 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">703 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Sweden and Novgorod make peace in the Treaty of Nöteborg</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the Novgorod Republic made peace at the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oreshek-fortress">Nöteborg</a>, at the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neva-river">Neva</a>. The treaty drew their first agreed border across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a>, leaving the western part with Sweden and the east with Novgorod.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-noteborg">Treaty of Nöteborg</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-novgorodian-wars">Swedish–Novgorodian Wars</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1759">1759 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">267 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Frederick the Great is crushed at Kunersdorf</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A Russian and Austrian army destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>'s Prussian force near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> an der Oder. Frederick wrote that all was lost, but his enemies failed to follow up and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> survived.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1851">1851 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">175 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Isaac Singer patents his improved sewing machine</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Isaac Singer received a US patent for a sewing machine with an up-and-down needle and foot treadle. Sold on instalment plans, Singer machines brought sewing machines into homes worldwide.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1944">1944 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">82 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">SS troops massacre villagers at Sant'Anna di Stazzema</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waffen-ss">Waffen-SS</a> soldiers killed some 560 civilians, many of them women and children, in the Tuscan village of Sant'Anna di Stazzema. It was one of the worst atrocities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-occupation-of-italy">German occupation of Italy</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-occupation-of-italy">German occupation of Italy</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1981">1981 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">45 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">IBM launches its Personal Computer</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">IBM unveiled the IBM PC model 5150, running Microsoft's MS-DOS. Its open design was widely copied, and 'PC compatible' machines came to dominate business and home computing.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/2000">2000 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">26 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Russian submarine Kursk sinks in the Barents Sea</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Explosions in its torpedo compartment sent the nuclear submarine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> to the seabed, killing all 118 crew. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s slow response and refusal of early foreign help caused national anger.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 12 August <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/christian-iii-of-denmark"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/christian-iii-of-denmark.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Christian III of Denmark</span><span class="tp">1503–1559</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemitsu"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/tokugawa-iemitsu.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Tokugawa Iemitsu</span><span class="tp">1604–1651</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/george-iv-of-the-united-kingdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/george-iv-of-the-united-kingdom.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">George IV of the United Kingdom</span><span class="tp">1762–1830</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mohammad-hatta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mohammad Hatta</span><span class="tp">1902–1980</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-zia-ul-haq"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muhammad-zia-ul-haq.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Muhammad Zia-ul-Haq</span><span class="tp">1924–1988</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 12 August <span class="n">6</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-ii-of-italy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/louis-ii-of-italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Louis II of Italy</span><span class="tp">c. 825–875</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/vladislaus-iii-duke-of-bohemia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/vladislaus-iii-duke-of-bohemia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Vladislaus III, Duke of Bohemia</span><span class="tp">d. 1222</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pope-sixtus-iv.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pope Sixtus IV</span><span class="tp">1414–1484</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abul-fazl-ibn-mubarak.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Abu'l-Fazl ibn Mubarak</span><span class="tp">1551–1602</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/william-blake"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/william-blake.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">William Blake</span><span class="tp">1757–1827</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/leos-janacek"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/leos-janacek.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Leoš Janáček</span><span class="tp">1854–1928</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
